<title>SWITZERLAND: Eurofima 1996 profit rises 25.5 pct.</title>
<headline>Eurofima 1996 profit rises 25.5 pct.</headline>
<text>
<p>BASLE, Switzerland, Jan 28 - Year to December 31, 1996</p>
<p>				    (million Sfr unless stated)</p>
<p> Net profit		     51.75	     vs 41.23</p>
<p> Cash flow			66.77	     vs 66.11</p>
<p> Total income		   1,844.9	   vs 1,882.8</p>
<p> Interest income		1,811.0	   vs 1,847.1</p>
<p> Total expenses		 1,793.1	   vs 1,841.5</p>
<p> Interest charges	     1,771.2	   vs 1,809.7</p>
<p> Total assets		   32,313.8	  vs 28,792.5</p>
<p>Rolling stock financed:</p>
<p> Locomotives, units	   248		 vs 171</p>
<p>Multiple-unit trains:</p>
<p>  Motor units		   303		 vs 286</p>
<p>  Trailer cars		  571		 vs 522</p>
<p> Passenger cars		 247		 vs 617</p>
<p> Freight cars		   1,028	     vs 801</p>
<p>NOTE - EUROFIMA, the European Company for the Financing of Railroad Rolling Stock, is owned by European railways and finances vehicles through borrowings or equity capital. EUROFIMA is headquartered in Basle.</p>
<p>-- Zurich Editorial +41 1 631 7340</p>
</text>
